# --- POWER9 hardware layer ---------------------------------------------------
# Omarchy's own hardware layer is Intel/NVIDIA/Apple/Dell/Framework and is
# clipped wholesale. What an AC922 or a Raptor board actually needs:
vulkan-radeon
lsp-plugins-lv2
powerpc-utils
opal-utils
ipmitool
alacritty

# Language servers and formatters for Neovim.
#
# omarchy-nvim ships lua/plugins/no-mason-downloads.lua, which turns off every
# automatic mason install -- the mason registry publishes x86_64 and aarch64
# assets only, so on ppc64le each one is a download that cannot succeed. That
# file's promise is that "every tool LazyVim's defaults reach for is packaged
# natively instead", and these are the packages that make it true. Without them
# the config disables mason and leaves nothing behind it: no LSP, no formatting,
# and a per-tool error on every start.
#
# clang (clangd) and tree-sitter-cli are listed elsewhere in this file.
#
# typescript-language-server needed pnpm, which needed pnpm. Bootstrapped via
# `npm install -g --prefix $srcdir/bootstrap pnpm` -- corepack was the obvious
# alternative but Arch's nodejs 26 no longer ships it.
#
# acorn is listed as a dependency of eslint rather than here: Arch's eslint
# deliberately deletes its bundled copy ("experimental dedup") and depends on
# the system package, which Arch POWER had never built.
#
# prettier needs oxc-parser only to BUILD -- its build imports every plugin to
# generate .d.ts files, and plugin-oxc pulls the native module. The shipped
# package contains no ELF and no .node at all, so arch=any is still right. The
# recipe builds oxc's napi cdylib for powerpc64le from source; oxc's published
# bindings.js already has a process.arch === 'ppc64' branch, so nothing needed
# patching, only providing.
lua-language-server
gopls
marksman
taplo-cli
bash-language-server
yaml-language-server
typescript-language-server
eslint
prettier
stylua
shfmt
ruff
rust-analyzer
